    "title": "How we know what happened",
    "contentNote": "Content note: this card names genocide, apartheid and other mass violence.",
    "key": "We can say what happened when courts, investigators and survivors agree on the evidence, and we can say who found it.",
    "body": "Genocide, apartheid and occupation are not only opinions. Courts hear evidence and give rulings. Independent investigators publish findings. Survivors give accounts that are checked against records. A fair account says who found what: a court's ruling, an investigators' report, an organization's conclusion. Some questions are still before a court. We can say what has been found, and we can also say plainly where we stand.",
    "tryIt": "When you read a claim about a war or a state, look for who found it: a court, a commission, researchers, or someone with a stake. Ask what each would count as evidence.",
    "where": "Public record: the International Court of Justice's advisory opinion of 19 July 2024, the report of the UN Independent International Commission of Inquiry of 16 September 2025, and the UN's definition of genocide. The habit of separating what happened from the story about it is from Rosenberg, Nonviolent Communication (2003). Paraphrased.",
    "more": "Some things are settled by courts and tribunals: the Holocaust, the genocide in Rwanda, and the genocide at Srebrenica. Others are widely recognized, such as the Armenian genocide. On Israel and Palestine, the International Court of Justice found in July 2024 that Israel's continued presence in the occupied Palestinian territory is unlawful and must end. Human Rights Watch, Amnesty International and the Israeli group B'Tselem have each concluded that Israeli authorities are committing the crime of apartheid against Palestinians. An independent United Nations commission, Amnesty International and Israeli human rights organizations have concluded that Israel has committed genocide in Gaza. The International Court of Justice has not yet ruled on that question, and the case will take years. Saying who found what is not hedging. It lets a reader check, and it keeps a true statement from being dismissed because it claimed too much.",
    "url": "https://www.icj-cij.org/",
